{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2279 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "052b17fa068042a02f5d487bc5c33dcf0e4b87e57d279ba25dcb95d7617048d1" :edb/db-before-t 2278 :edb/db-after-t 2279 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535591 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:09:46.912Z" 13194139535591 true] [17592186166465 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/d8be54eddd0358742170a03b3c55e0cb94eaf2a7d59f12e1c4361a6197d29847.png)\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2?$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186166465 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\ln 2$?" 13194139535591 false] [17592186166465 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(\\ln 2) &= e^{3(\\ln 2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2e^{\\ln 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 2 \\cdot 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\ln 2) &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2(12) + 1(4) \\\\ 1(12) + 2(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}24 + 4 \\\\ 12 + 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535591 false] [17592186166465 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot 2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186166466 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." 13194139535591 true] [17592186166466 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) - y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space." 13194139535591 false] [17592186166466 1145 "Recall that:\n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$3x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = 3x$\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n$-2x-y=0 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad y = -2x$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/329339a73bbba31c17772216e1329304.png)" 13194139535591 false] [17592186166466 1145 "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which $x' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n3x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = 3x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which $y' = 0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n-2x-y=0 \n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\ny = -2x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/66cdd05ac68d762c5b78df40b22f9e0cd660099c13e48e9eb71c81c73a044280.png)" 13194139535591 true] [17592186166467 1100 "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/0cf7942e618f0465ead5b98c6404a3747e505db75517725971c833c97121ab64.png)" 13194139535591 true] [17592186166467 1100 "A sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown below (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Sketch the trajectory of the solution starting at the marked point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/d259112c09bd560643afeb7ef35f3134.png)" 13194139535591 false] [17592186166467 1145 "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/105bdbdcac5f0ff20f71a0131bf5bb3cbafe0e93ce8bc733a51c2dcb3fb8364f.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/96757a3aee3b161b2ba9887cd85f977f063807779604647dd791cf265790cabc.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/658c15d2291368d0e6836ba19b90157d49dee4a510d58cddf7f85ddc9ff72a8d.png)" 13194139535591 true] [17592186166467 1145 "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, moving along with the flow (downward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/1e72cfbafb65f970d697dda4103fc713.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues downward, but the trajectory starts to turn slightly to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/77ad3d8eb22bdba17fcf14e48052927e.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to go downwards and slightly to the left to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/a442afec461d42b693fbda69339f3689.png)" 13194139535591 false] [17592186166841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356544 13194139535591 true] [17592186166841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356551 13194139535591 true] [17592186166841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356558 13194139535591 true] [17592186166841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356565 13194139535591 true] [17592186166841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356572 13194139535591 true] [17592186166842 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356579 13194139535591 true] [17592186166842 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356590 13194139535591 true] [17592186166842 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356601 13194139535591 true] [17592186166842 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356612 13194139535591 true] [17592186166842 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356623 13194139535591 true] [17592186166843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356634 13194139535591 true] [17592186166843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356641 13194139535591 true] [17592186166843 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356648 13194139535591 true] [17592186167166 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535591 true] [17592186167166 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/14ed16cc149ae777e195aa59e39cf809e939032a925e7acbd88cd685af99dd0e.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" 13194139535591 true] [17592186167166 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3188/Source/Images/q-332920.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" 13194139535591 false] [17592186167166 1145 "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (straight to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/0d010be8bad72bf4ebac4dbe2d325f1a1f194d78c676e2a62306b5c7d2af3c66.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going to the right  but then starts turning downward.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/5118424696b9602f10bf116c3a292e2fb58ed2f9acd2a9507888ab92a9b8986b.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to move rightward and bend downward, converging to the origin.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/92a58573efafd7155cf02faf52e27ab968d9161ad448530b45673fd83a61fde1.png)" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356544 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356545 13194139535591 true] [17592186356544 1089 "q-332607" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356544 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535591 true] [17592186356544 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356544 1113 #uuid "6c25cae4-5e98-5fb9-80f0-9786e8d8a695" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356544 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-3x+y \\\\  & =-3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x+4y \\\\  & =2(-1)+4(2) \\\\  & =-2+8 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356546 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356547 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356548 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356549 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356550 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1041 "selection" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356548 13194139535591 true] [17592186356545 1110 #uuid "332208d8-cbb8-5439-8cd9-3d7092749f63" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356546 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356546 1111 #uuid "2dd17ac0-f812-5fc8-a169-5f187a942545" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356547 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356547 1111 #uuid "98ffdb4e-121c-5e5e-ab54-3194ebb64839" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356548 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356548 1111 #uuid "df2c57e2-7db9-5c0b-b79b-190ecbb971b8" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356549 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356549 1111 #uuid "3b8787fa-b703-56c6-8c95-50dea1c67e9d" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356550 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356550 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356550 1111 #uuid "e2ff297a-fa5e-52f2-83c3-895b460968a2" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356551 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356552 13194139535591 true] [17592186356551 1089 "q-332612" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356551 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535591 true] [17592186356551 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356551 1113 #uuid "0edb7bdb-b6a4-50c3-b4ac-463fffe106e6" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356551 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =2x+y \\\\  & =2(-1)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-x+3y \\\\  & =-(-1)+3(-2) \\\\  & =1-6 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356553 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356554 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356555 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356556 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356557 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1041 "selection" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356557 13194139535591 true] [17592186356552 1110 #uuid "54f5e225-d732-5dd7-b910-3aefc1c03bab" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356553 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356553 1111 #uuid "d81bc0e4-ffac-50bf-b538-22ce65da5230" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356554 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356554 1111 #uuid "ecbb6ede-1585-560e-855a-5223ec215d8a" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356555 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356555 1111 #uuid "9708421d-745e-5d9e-b394-beb3b271025a" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356556 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356556 1111 #uuid "4b176b4c-bf2d-5108-be11-b6c695c84be5" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356557 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356557 1111 #uuid "4bc57c5b-4ac1-52c7-af25-ca17f988dec8" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356558 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356559 13194139535591 true] [17592186356558 1089 "q-332614" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356558 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535591 true] [17592186356558 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(0,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356558 1113 #uuid "c316bd8b-521b-5177-a038-0d7ba4ee45c6" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356558 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =3x-2y \\\\  & =3(0)-2(-2) \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-4x+y \\\\  & =-4(0)+(-2) \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356560 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356561 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356562 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356563 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356564 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1041 "selection" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356563 13194139535591 true] [17592186356559 1110 #uuid "1e04ea8d-6e65-5782-b4f2-ff8e54b410df" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356560 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356560 1111 #uuid "0c6dfaea-b072-53f7-9c5c-d8624acbdc18" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356561 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356561 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356561 1111 #uuid "ebb4692f-4c8d-58a2-be12-d8fbacbc2773" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356562 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356562 1111 #uuid "a5adf7ad-5e77-57b7-8e35-f06ce56576cd" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356563 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356563 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356563 1111 #uuid "0f765c89-7221-5f96-ba05-085952c1bd9e" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356564 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356564 1111 #uuid "bc54e4e3-3336-5f61-8c25-2c83020e4a91" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356565 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356566 13194139535591 true] [17592186356565 1089 "q-332616" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535591 true] [17592186356565 1100 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356565 1113 #uuid "28e64722-99a7-5d4a-b749-394a97d4ce0b" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356565 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356567 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356568 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356569 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356570 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356571 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1041 "selection" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356570 13194139535591 true] [17592186356566 1110 #uuid "96beea68-0cd0-553a-865f-53377d322948" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356567 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356567 1111 #uuid "379038b0-c445-55e4-982d-ec8ebd3ad714" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356568 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 48\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356568 1111 #uuid "84bb893d-40e1-530e-9409-ec378e47918d" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356569 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356569 1111 #uuid "aa251ca4-4b76-5ee3-9e5a-ff5a435aa9e9" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356570 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 192\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356570 1111 #uuid "1bba3760-918b-5641-a3cd-bac39c0b9ba9" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356571 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}192 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356571 1111 #uuid "6f4d3749-8bf9-5af8-94c9-2ba0bdd462bf" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356572 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356573 13194139535591 true] [17592186356572 1089 "q-332619" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535591 true] [17592186356572 1100 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356572 1113 #uuid "be9877ee-3abb-5c56-bc3e-693bac7c1ef7" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356572 1145 "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2(\\frac{1}{3})+0(6) \\\\ 0(\\frac{1}{3})+1(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 131