356579 1089 "q-332643" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356579 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535591 true] [17592186356579 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535591 true] [17592186356579 1113 #uuid "27897490-4a2b-5700-b06d-4c9a02df7ee3" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356579 1145 "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-4x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=4x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068149/assets/1c701d81ab131757758e19c3c1d9813f1e8cda3ff069843c9105dd125d1caf97.png)" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356581 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356582 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356583 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356584 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1041 "field-1" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356583 13194139535591 true] [17592186356580 1110 #uuid "d7f9ba7d-b3e9-55a5-a803-74354ba173b9" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356581 1034 "-4x" 13194139535591 true] [17592186356581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535591 true] [17592186356581 1111 #uuid "d4e0432c-6118-5d74-b53b-14